[THOUGHT] Heavy, long-term smoking (≥40 pack-years) + unintentional weight loss + progressive painful arm weakness is highly concerning for a **Pancoast tumor** (apical lung cancer), which commonly presents with ipsilateral shoulder/arm pain, Horner’s syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis), and brachial plexus involvement — especially lower trunk (C8–T1), but upper trunk (C5–C7) involvement can cause isolated arm weakness. Though classic Pancoast symptoms include hand muscle atrophy or Horner’s, early or atypical presentations may lack those. This now becomes the leading diagnostic consideration. Next: **Targeted physical examination** is essential to detect objective signs — particularly Horner’s syndrome, muscle atrophy, fasciculations, sensory deficits in dermatomes/myotomes, and brachial plexus tenderness. I must request this before proceeding to labs/imaging.

[TEST] Perform targeted physical examination including:  
- Assessment for Horner’s syndrome (ptosis, miosis, facial anhidrosis) on the right side;  
- Inspection and palpation of right arm for muscle atrophy (deltoid, biceps, triceps, thenar eminence);  
- Manual muscle testing of right shoulder abduction (C5), elbow flexion (C6), wrist extension (C7), and finger extension (C8);  
- Sensory testing in C5–T1 dermatomes (lateral arm, thumb, index/middle fingers, medial arm/hand);  
- Palpation of supraclavicular and axillary lymph nodes;  
- Evaluation for cervical spine tenderness or radicular pain on Spurling’s maneuver.

Please report all findings.

